{
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "metadata": {
    "note": "The Syracuse Northern Railroad, chartered 1868 and opened November 9 1871, gave Syracuse its first direct rail line north to Sandy Creek in Oswego County (about 35 miles), via Brewerton, Central Square, Parish, Pulaski, and Lacona -- roughly paralleling today's US Route 11. It struggled financially from the start and was foreclosed and leased to the Rome, Watertown and Ogdensburg Railroad (RW&O) in 1875 (see watertown_rome_railroad.geojson for the rest of that system), which gave the RW&O a second direct connection to the New York Central main line at Syracuse alongside its existing Rome connection. The northernmost ~8 miles, Pulaski through Lacona to Sandy Creek, was torn up by 1878, leaving Pulaski as the line's permanent northern terminus. The whole RW&O system (including this branch) was leased to the New York Central in 1891. In 1885 a related but distinct company, the Syracuse, Phoenix and Oswego Railway, revived part of the original Syracuse Northern's Syracuse-Woodard's trackage as the start of a new Syracuse-Fulton-Oswego route (the 'Hojack' branch of the RW&O's St. Lawrence Division) -- that separate 1885 line is not traced on this map. Geometry for the Syracuse-Sandy Creek route is real-traced from OpenStreetMap; every hop resolved to a connected, plausible real track path with no straight-line fallbacks needed anywhere on the route.",
    "sources": [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syracuse_Northern_Railroad (incorporated 1868, opened November 9 1871, Syracuse to Watertown trade area, extended to Pulaski and Lacona 1875, merged into Syracuse and Northern Railroad then into the Rome, Watertown and Ogdensburg Railroad, both in 1875)",
      "Grokipedia 'Syracuse Northern Railroad' article, citing Town of Clay NY historian's office, Landmarks of Oswego County (archive.org), and other local-history sources (chartered February 25 1868; organized early 1870 with $1,250,000 capital stock; construction from Saxon Street, Salina; bridges at Parish, Brewerton, and Pulaski (Salmon River); opened November 9 1871 Syracuse-Sandy Creek, ~35 miles; two express + one accommodation passenger train each direction, two freight trains; foreclosure and sheriff's sale August 1875; leased to RW&O 1875, which built a tunnel and Oswego River bridge (completed 1876) and made it part of the Western Division headquartered at Oswego; Pulaski-Lacona-Sandy Creek segment (~8 miles) abandoned by 1878; 1885 revival as the Syracuse, Phoenix and Oswego Railway/'Hojack' branch, reusing Woodard's-Syracuse trackage and building 17 new miles Woodard's-Fulton plus NY,O&W trackage rights Fulton-Oswego; whole RW&O system leased to New York Central March 14 1891)"
    ],
    "geometry_sources": [
      "OpenStreetMap (via Overpass API, overpass.openstreetmap.fr mirror): railway=rail (active) combined with railway=abandoned/disused tagging, Syracuse to Sandy Creek via Brewerton, Central Square, Parish, Pulaski, and Lacona; routed via Dijkstra shortest path between each station's geocoded coordinates. All hops resolved to connected, plausible paths (distance ratios within ~1.2x of straight-line) with no fallbacks needed."
    ],
    "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for the RW&O and NYC eras; the precise passenger-service end date for the Pulaski branch (1932 used here is an unsourced placeholder, not a documented date); the 1885 Syracuse, Phoenix and Oswego Railway / Hojack revival route (Woodard's-Fulton-Oswego) is not traced on this map.",
    "pulled": "20260824",
    "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."
  },
