            "note": "Chartered February 25 1868, organized in early 1870 with $1,250,000 capital stock (Syracuse subscribed $250,000 in bonds); the idea itself dated back to 1851. Construction ran from Saxon Street in Salina, with bridges at Parish, Brewerton, and a high steel-and-stone-arch crossing of the Salmon River at Pulaski. Opened November 9 1871, Syracuse to Sandy Creek in Oswego County, about 35 miles, with two daily express trains and one accommodation train each way, plus two daily freight trains carrying Syracuse salt, lumber, and farm products. Never met its promoters' financial expectations; foreclosed and sold at a sheriff's sale in Onondaga County in August 1875. Frequency from a real 1871-era timetable detail (two express + one accommodation train each direction).",
